\section{Free-Form Decoding Ablation}\label{app:freeform_ablation}

NER exhibits oracle scaling ($c{\approx}0.5$; \S\ref{sec:scaling}), but degeneracy (17.8--67.9\%) and LP-quality alignment ($\rho$ 0.043--0.667) constrain aggregation; temperature disentangles these at $T{=}1.2$ (Table~\ref{tab:t_degeneracy}).

\paragraph{Degeneracy governs the selection gap across model families.}
Across four frontier models on Few-NERD ($N{=}8$), degeneracy and the selection gap (oracle${-}$MV F1) are negatively correlated: DeepSeek (5.5\% degen, 0.290 gap), Gemini 2.5 Pro (28.5\%, 0.113), GPT-5.5 (50\%, 0.068), Claude Sonnet~4 (74\%, 0.032).
Higher degeneracy eliminates the instances where aggregation could help, compressing the gap regardless of model capability.
This pattern complements the within-pipeline degeneracy bound (\S\ref{sec:theory}): the bottleneck is structural, not model-specific (Table~\ref{tab:frontier_sc_cross}).

\paragraph{From diagnosis to practice.}\label{sec:selection_method}

